          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A biopsy from a woman with linear, branching microcalcifications on mammography </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A biopsy from a woman with linear, branching microcalcifications on mammography shows ducts filled with high-grade cells with central necrosis. The myoepithelial layer is preserved. What is the best interpretation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lobular carcinoma in situ, managed by surveillance</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>LCIS shows small, uniform, discohesive cells filling the TDLU with E-cadherin loss. It does not produce comedo-type necrosis with linear calcifications.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Comedo-type ductal carcinoma in situ, treated with excision and often radiation</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>High-grade cells with central necrosis that calcifies give the linear and branching microcalcifications of comedo DCIS. It is a localised precursor with the highest risk of progression, treated with excision and often radiation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Invasive ductal carcinoma of no special type</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Invasive ductal carcinoma breaches the basement membrane and destroys the myoepithelial layer. Here the myoepithelial layer is preserved, so the process is in situ.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Paget disease of the nipple</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Paget disease shows large pale cells within the nipple epidermis. It is not a duct-filling process with central necrosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Preserved myoepithelial cells mean the disease is in situ. High-grade cells with central necrosis and linear, branching calcification point to comedo DCIS, which is treated with excision and often radiation. LCIS is a bilateral risk marker managed by surveillance."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. A preserved myoepithelial layer and intact basement membrane define in situ disease, and comedo necrosis with calcification is the high-risk DCIS pattern."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
