          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 44-year-old woman has a rapidly growing 6 cm breast lump. On biopsy, the strom</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 44-year-old woman has a rapidly growing 6 cm breast lump. On biopsy, the stroma is markedly hypercellular and forms leaf-like fronds projecting into cystic spaces. Which finding best separates this lesion from a fibroadenoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypercellular stroma with leaf-like fronds</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Stromal cellularity separates phyllodes tumour from fibroadenoma. Fibroadenoma has pale, hypocellular stroma, whereas phyllodes has hypercellular stroma in leaf-like fronds.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ducts compressed into slit-like spaces by stroma</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the intracanalicular pattern, which is seen in fibroadenoma. It does not distinguish phyllodes tumour from fibroadenoma.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A grey-white, encapsulated, rubbery nodule</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the gross appearance of fibroadenoma. Phyllodes tumour is usually larger, with a leaf-like cut surface.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Single-file rows of discohesive cells with E-cadherin loss</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes invasive lobular carcinoma and is unrelated to the fibroepithelial lesions being compared.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The discriminator is the stroma. Fibroadenoma has pale, hypocellular stroma, while phyllodes tumour has hypercellular stroma in leaf-like fronds. Slit-like ducts and encapsulation occur in fibroadenoma."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Stromal cellularity with leaf-like fronds identifies phyllodes tumour. A rapidly growing fibroadenoma-like lump in a woman over 35 must be biopsied, not observed."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
